==German==

===Etymology===
{{prefix|de|auf|machen}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈaʊ̯fˌmaxən/|[ˈʔäo̯fˌmäχən]|[-χn̩]|[-χɴ̩]}}
* {{audio|de|De-aufmachen.ogg}}
* {{audio|de|De-aufmachen2.ogg}}
* {{audio|de|De-at-aufmachen.ogg|a=Austria}}
* {{hyph|de|auf|ma|chen}}
* {{rhymes|de|axn̩|s=3}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|auf.machen}}

# {{lb|de|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[open]] (a container, a door or window)
#: {{ux|de|'''Mach''' mir '''auf'''!|'''Open''' [the door] for me.}}
#: {{ux|de|Kannst du dieses Glas '''aufmachen'''?|Can you '''open''' this jar?}}
# {{lb|de|transitive|or|intransitive}} to open for business
#: {{ux|de|Ich liebe den neuen Laden, der kürzlich '''aufgemacht''' hat.|I love the new store that '''opened''' recently.}}
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to get ready; to [[set out]]
# {{lb|de|dated|colloquial}} to [[put up]]; to [[hang]] {{q|curtains, a poster etc.}}

====Usage notes====
* {{m|de||Aufmachen}} and {{m|de|zumachen}} are not in themselves informal. However, the formal language prefers {{m|de|öffnen}} and {{m|de|schließen}}, which are in turn rare in colloquial speech (though {{m|de||schließen}} is common in compounds like {{m|de|abschließen}}, {{m|de|zuschließen}}, etc.).

